# SYNOPSIS

```usage
dc [-i] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`dc` is a reverse-Polish calculator on decimal numbers of any size.
It runs the commands in each <file> in turn, then those on standard input; a <file> of `-` is standard input.

A number is pushed on the stack; it may have a fraction, and a leading `_` makes it negative.
With an input base above 10, `A` to `F` are digits 10 to 15.
The commands are single characters, and blanks between them are ignored:

: `p`
  Print the top of the stack, and a newline, leaving it there.
: `n`
  Pop the top and print it, with no newline.
: `P`
  Pop the top and print it with no newline: a string as it is, a number as described in BUGS.
: `f`
  Print the whole stack, top first, one value to a line.
: `+` `-` `*` `/` `%` `^`
  Pop two values and push their sum, difference, product, quotient, remainder or power; the second popped is the left operand.
  The quotient has `scale` digits after the point; a power's exponent is taken as an integer.
: `~`
  Pop two values and push their quotient, then their remainder.
: `v`
  Pop a value and push its square root, to `scale` digits after the point.
: `c`, `d`, `r`, `z`
  Clear the stack; push a copy of the top; swap the top two; push the stack's depth.
: `s`<x>, `l`<x>
  Pop the top into register <x>, or push a copy of register <x>.
: `S`<x>, `L`<x>
  Push the top onto register <x>'s own stack, or pop that stack onto the main one.
: `:`<x>, `;`<x>
  Pop an index, then a value, and store the value at that index of array <x>; or pop an index and push the value stored there.
: `i`, `o`, `k`
  Pop the input base (2 to 16), the output base (2 or more) or the scale (0 or more).
: `I`, `O`, `K`
  Push the input base, the output base or the scale.
: `[`<string>`]`
  Push <string>; brackets inside it nest, or are escaped with `\`.
: `x`
  Pop the top and run it as commands if it is a string; a number is pushed back.
: `>`<x>, `<`<x>, `=`<x>, `!>`<x>, `!<`<x>, `!=`<x>
  Pop two values and run register <x> if the top is greater than the second, less, equal, not greater, not less, or not equal.
: `?`
  Read a line of standard input and push it as a string; `?x` runs it.
: `q`
  Leave the string being run and the one that ran it; at the outer level, exit.
: `Q`
  Pop <n> and leave <n> levels of strings being run.
: `Z`, `X`
  Pop a value and push its number of digits, or a string's length; or its number of fraction digits, 0 for a string.
: `!`<command>
  Run the rest of the line with `system`; see BUGS.
: `#`
  Ignore the rest of the line.

The flag is:

: `-i`
  Allow register names of more than one character: `<`<n>`>` names register number <n>, and `"`<name>`"` any name.

`dc` is sbase's, a hard link to `/boot/bin/posix/sbase-box`; there is no native one.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it as `/bin/dc`, and bc(1) runs it.

