# DIAGNOSTICS

A command that fails, such as one on an empty stack, is reported on standard error, as `dc: stack empty`, and `dc` goes on with the next command.
Dividing by 0 is reported as `dc: divide by 0`.
An unknown flag prints `usage: dc [-i] [file ...]` and exits 1, and a <file> that cannot be opened is reported, as `dc: opening '/x': No such file or directory`, and exits 1.
Otherwise the exit status is 0, whatever failed.

# BUGS

`!` cannot run anything on VectraOS: `system` needs `/bin/sh`, which there is not.

`P` of a number does not print the bytes traditional `dc` does: it prints the integer part in base 100, each digit as a space and two decimal digits, so `65P` prints ` 65`, not `A`.

Where POSIX gives a square root the scale of its operand when that is larger, `v` gives `scale` digits only.

Standard input is always read after the <file>s, unless one of them quits.

With `-i`, a register number above 255 wraps: `<300>` is the register `,`.

sbase's own page has the comparisons backwards, and says `?` runs the line it reads; the source does as this page says.
